What stands between us and taking money, in order

A runbook. `PAYMENTS-CHECKLIST.md`, excluded from the build like every other
internal document, listing everything left before FCPM can take a payment on
its own website — and who can do each piece.

No code changes. This is the thing to read before touching any of it.

## Why it exists

The Stripe machinery here is finished and switched off, and those two facts
look identical from outside. `_data/payments.yml` explains beautifully *why*
each piece is the way it is, across 240 lines, and answers "what is left"
nowhere. That question came up today and took me an hour of reading to answer.

## The headline

**The machinery is done.** The broker is written, 119 tests pass, prices
generate from `_data/` and CI fails if the two disagree, repricing existing
subscribers is written, the nonprofit half-rate cannot be self-claimed.
Nothing needs designing.

What is missing is **four values, one namespace, one page, and two board
decisions** — and one build step that is genuinely ours.

## Three things I found that I do not think were known

**1. The KV namespace has no id.** `worker/wrangler.jsonc` declares a binding
called `CHALLENGES` with `"id": ""`. A binding with no namespace behind it
cannot deploy, so this blocks the broker regardless of tokens. Adding
`CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` without also running `wrangler kv namespace create`
just moves the failure one step later.

**2. `/membership/` is publishing the word TODO right now.** `membership.md`
renders `tier.summary` unconditionally, and three of the four tiers say
`TODO`. That is live, today, on the public site. I have deliberately **not**
fixed it here — it is a one-line guard and it belongs with the buy-button
work, not in a documentation PR that would then conflict with it.

**3. Nothing on the site calls the broker.** `POST /checkout` is implemented,
tested and routed, and no page, include or script sends it anything. So even
with every key in place, a visitor could not pay. The contract is in the file.
`/thanks/` — the default success page — does not exist either.

## What I did not do

- **I set no price, picked no provider, and wrote no tier benefit.** Those are
  board decisions; `ADVOCATE.md` and the data files both say a plausible guess
  is worse than a blank, and I agree.
- **I did not build the buy button.** It is the one remaining item that is
  ours rather than yours, it touches money, and it deserves its own review.
  Say the word and it is the next PR.
- **I did not touch `_data/payments.yml`.** Nothing in it is wrong.

## For the board meeting

If one thing gets decided today, the cheapest with the most unblocked behind
it is **2.3, the class drop-in prices** — two numbers. `build-prices.py`
already refuses to sell an unpriced thing, so those two figures are the whole
blocker on class sales.
